    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Виктор Петрович, вы живы?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Viktor Petrovich, are you alive?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'Viktor' with 'living'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "А?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Huh?' Chef Viktor asks this with a single word meaning 'and', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ой, вы простите меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, please forgive me.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'oh' with 'me'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ради бога, простите.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For God's sake, forgive me.' Notice how 'for the sake of' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'excuse me'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я вас просто не заметила.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I just didn't see you.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'to notice'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Так вы расправляетесь с конкурентами.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So this is how you deal with the competition.' Chef Viktor uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'rival', keeping a formal or respectful register.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дайте, я вас приподниму.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here, let me help you up.' Elena combines 'give' with 'lift slightly' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поедем в больницу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will go to the hospital.' Elena links 'will be off' directly with 'the hospital' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Мне больно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It hurts.' Notice how 'to me' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'it hurts'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надо ехать к врачу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We need to go to the doctor.' Notice how 'it is necessary' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'the doctor'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ладно, поехали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'All right, let's go.' Chef Viktor combines 'all right' with 'here we go' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Elena responds.",
